# SPINAS

SPINAS (Spinor Amplitudes) is a software package for calculating constructive scattering amplitudes.

## Prerequisites

SPINAS requires:
* CMake (version 3.10 or higher)
* A C++11 compiler and its standard library

The library and its validation tests require no additional packages.

## Unix-like Systems (Linux, macOS, WSL, Cygwin, etc.)

### Compilation

To compile and install SPINAS, open a terminal and follow these steps:
```
mkdir build
cd build
cmake ..
make -j10
```

### Running Tests

After installation, you can run the provided tests:
```
./test_spinas
./test_SM
```

`test_spinas` checks the component algebra, spinor chains, cached updates,
spin-1 state normalization and Lorentz transformations. `test_SM` runs the
Standard Model process validations.

To run both validation suites together and show details if a test fails:
```
ctest --output-on-failure
```

Each test executable prints its random seed. To replay a run, supply that seed:
```
./test_spinas --seed 12345
./test_SM --seed 12345
```

The shared testing utility is kept in `tests/test_utils.h` and
`tests/test_utils.cpp`. Failed checks report their location and return a
nonzero exit status, including in optimized builds. To build only the libraries,
configure with `cmake -DBUILD_TESTING=OFF ..`.

### Sharing momentum insertions between spinor products

The usual `sproduct::update()` interface remains available. If several brackets
contain the same inserted momentum sequence, a process can instead own one
`momentum_chain` and reuse its matrix:

```cpp
// The external particle objects must already exist.
spinas::momentum_chain middle(ANGLE, {&p1, &p2});
spinas::sproduct a3124a(ANGLE, &p3, &p1, &p2, &p4);
spinas::sproduct a5126a(ANGLE, &p5, &p1, &p2, &p6);

// After assigning particle momenta, and after every subsequent mass/momentum update:
middle.update();
a3124a.update(middle);
a5126a.update(middle);
```

The helper alternates lower and upper momentum matrices in the same order as
an ordinary bracket. The shared update checks the exact particle sequence,
length and angle/square orientation, and rejects a helper that has not been
updated. Its cached matrix is owned by that helper; no global cache is used.
Keep the particle objects alive and at stable addresses, as for ordinary
`sproduct` objects. Separate processes or worker threads should own separate
particles and helpers. A copied process must rebind these pointers to its own
particle members, just as it already does for its ordinary spinor products.

Ordinary particle setup obtains spinor phases directly from Cartesian momentum
components, including stable subnormal directions and signed z-axis choices.
Spherical angles are evaluated lazily when angular generators or diagnostics
request them. Rest-state particle constructors initialize their spinor and
momentum-matrix caches immediately.

After updating these class definitions, rebuild SPINAS and any application that
links it. The installed headers and library must describe the same class layout.

### Installation

If you would like to install the libraries, you can run:
```
sudo make install
```

You can specify a custom install location by setting the `CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX` option:

```
cmake -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/path/to/install/location ..
make install
```

Replace `/path/to/install/location` with your desired installation directory.

##Windows

### Compilation

For native Windows compilation, you need to have CMake and a compiler (like Visual Studio) installed.

Open a Command Prompt or PowerShell window and navigate to your project directory, then follow these steps:
```
mkdir build
cd build
cmake ..
cmake --build . --config Release
```

### Running Tests

After installation, you can run the provided tests:
```
test_spinas.exe
test_SM.exe
```

### Installation

If you would like to install the libraries, you can run:
```
cmake --install .
```

You can specify a custom install location by setting the `CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX` option:
```
cmake -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=C:\path\to\install\location ..
cmake --install .
```

Replace `C:\path\to\install\location` with your desired installation directory.

## Usage

### Including SPINAS in your project

To use SPINAS in your project, include the `spinas.h` header file in your source code:
```
#include <spinas/spinas.h>
```

Then, when compiling your project, link against the SPINAS library. The name of the library
is libspinas.so on Unix-like systems and spinas.lib on Windows. If you installed SPINAS in
a non-default location, make sure to tell your compiler where to find the headers and the
library file. For example, if you're using GCC, you can do this:
```
g++ -I/path/to/spinas/include -L/path/to/spinas/lib -lspinas your_source_file.cpp
```

Replace /path/to/spinas with the location where you installed SPINAS. On Windows, you would
modify the include and library directories in your project settings if you're using an IDE
like Visual Studio.

## Numerical evaluation

A `particle` carries an external on-shell mass together with its momentum.
Positive-energy spinors and momentum matrices use that mass to evaluate small
components without subtracting nearly equal energy and momentum values. For
example, `sqrt(E-|p|)` is evaluated as `abs(m)/sqrt(E+|p|)`. The declared mass
remains important even when floating-point energy and momentum round to the
same number. The momentum check allows arithmetic roundoff and still rejects
resolved off-shell inputs; it does not modify the supplied four-vector.

The general momentum-matrix constructor and propagator denominator also accept
virtual momenta and do not apply this external mass-shell substitution. Rebuild
SPINAS after changing its source and relink executables using the static library
to use the updated numerical routines.

## Examples

For examples of how to use this library, see the SM directory, where some SM processes are
calculated using the library and don't forget to read the manual.

